ng advantage while DiLorenzo's account is thin after a costly primary", "Late-September stories question DiLorenzo's financial disclosures", "Only one public poll (UNH, D+4) leaves real uncertainty in a wide-open race"], "rationale": "A favorable national environment, a Likely D consensus among raters, and markets pricing a Democratic win around 93-95% make Stefany Shaheen the clear favorite, though the single UNH poll shows a narrower four-point contest and the open, lightly Democratic seat keeps this from Safe territory.", "watch": "If new polling confirms the mid-September UNH margin it would firm this up, but if DiLorenzo closes amid the disclosure controversy or outspends expectations, the race could shift toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chris Pappas (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Hampshire's First District opens up this fall with Chris Pappas running for Senate instead of seeking reelection, leaving both parties to contest a seat that leans only slightly Democratic. Stefany Shaheen, daughter of a Democratic senator, survived a competitive primary, while Republican Anthony DiLorenzo, who emphasizes working-class roots, secured his nomination.\n\nMost race raters call the district Likely Democratic, with a few at Lean Democratic. The one public poll, from the University of New Hampshire, has Shaheen ahead 46 to 42 among likely voters. Markets give her roughly a 93 to 95 percent chance of winning. She also holds a clear money advantage, with super PAC support and a larger war chest, while DiLorenzo enters the general election with limited cash and faces Democratic attacks over his financial disclosures. The forecast makes Shaheen a favorite by about seven points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.937, "margin": 10.01}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9238, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9245, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8928, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09